<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antigua Labour Party</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Antigua and Barbuda Labour Party</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Antigua Labour Party, later renamed the Antigua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> Labour Party, grew out of the Antigua Trades and Labour Union in the 1940s. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vere-bird">Vere Bird</a> and later his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lester-bird">Lester Bird</a> it dominated Antiguan politics, leading the country to independence in 1981 and governing until 2004. It returned to power under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-browne">Gaston Browne</a> in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antigua-labour-party.jpg" alt="Antigua Labour Party" width="320" height="370" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LadyofHats, based on a bitmap by Ralf Hartemink · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_Antigua_and_Barbuda.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded in 1946, at the time of the first national elections, as the Antigua Labour Party. Its founders included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vere-bird">Vere Bird</a>, and it came to be seen as the political arm of the Bird family. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lester-bird">Lester Bird</a> was party chairman from 1971 and became prime minister and political leader in 1994. Apart from five years in the 1970s, the party governed for more than four decades up to 2004.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Two developments preceded its defeat in 2004, its first in 28 years. Commonwealth observers had found the 1999 election marked by irregularities and inflated voting rolls, and an Electoral Commission was introduced afterwards; in 2002 a scandal broke over medical benefits, when the government failed to contribute EC$120 million to cover recipients' medical expenses. The opposition United Progressive Party campaigned against corruption under the slogan Government in the Sunshine. In the election of 24 March 2004 the Labour Party won 41.8 percent of the vote but only four of the 17 seats, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lester-bird">Lester Bird</a> lost his own constituency.</p><p>Bird had said he would stand down if the party lost the 2009 election; it lost, but he remained leader until 2012, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-browne">Gaston Browne</a> defeated him in a leadership challenge. Under Browne the party won 14 of the 17 seats in 2014 and formed a majority government, and it kept power with 15 seats in 2018.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Although it grew from the labour movement and has social democratic roots, the party is generally considered economically liberal and fiscally conservative, favouring a market economy and lower taxes and rejecting an income tax. Because its governments kept the public sector as the largest employer, at times with up to 40 percent of the workforce, some have called its policy paternalistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservatism">conservatism</a>. After the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a> it officially supported making Antigua and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbuda">Barbuda</a> a republic. Browne's government appointed Aziza Lake, the first known LGBTQ rights activist in the Senate, and did not challenge a court ruling of 2023 that legalised same-sex sexual activity, which caused tensions within the party.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antigua and Barbuda Labour Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vere Bird, the trade unionist who had led the <b>Antigua Labour Party</b> since the 1940s, became the first prime minister, with the British monarch as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/antigua-and-barbuda">Antigua and Barbuda</a></li></ul></section>
